    "original_text": "Read-only inspection of the working-tree candidate (no tests or git run; tree/manifest hash identity not independently recomputed). Core repairs are substantively correct: explicit package roles with producer-pinned source-RPM path and bounded RPM header type verification; presence checks use is_installable_binary only; src/nosrc not added to architecture sets; source evidence bound exactly by name/size/sha to reproducibility declarations; promotion restrictions computed in an independent guarded step and the conservative union enforced in validate_summary; centralized family/format->canonical adapter mapping (deb->apt, rpm->dnf, pacman->pacman) failing closed with UNKNOWN_ADAPTER, ADAPTER_GATES unchanged with no aliases, destination id/revision scheme/qualification scope preserved, bounded partial results on later failure consumed by hosted_pipeline; classify_tls_error now checks typed SSL exceptions before unwrapping, with bounded cycle-safe unwrapping (max 4 levels) and bounded verify_code; pages_origin keeps CERT_REQUIRED + check_hostname, refuses redirects, failure=unknown, absent only on exact 404 over verified TLS. Findings (advisory): (1) MEDIUM: hosted_observe.execute unconditionally calls diagnose_pages_origin(path='CNAME') (src/rs9/hosted_observe.py:388-389), but most HostedObserveExecutionTests patch read_pages and not diagnose_pages_origin (only tests/test_hosted_observe.py:1130 does); e.g. test_failure_g_real_record_shape_regression (lines 310-320) therefore performs a live DNS child process, TCP, TLS and HTTP GET to rs9.knowledge-forge.ai during the unit suite. Tests pass offline because states are not asserted, but the suite has outbound side effects and latency, and the reported qualification run probably attempted network access. Patch it in class setUp or inject it. (2) LOW-MEDIUM: retain() raises CUSTODY_ROLE for any unclassified package (src/rs9/hosted_custody.py:122-126) and hosted_pipeline calls retain outside its try/partial-result boundary (src/rs9/hosted_pipeline.py:231), so one unexpected package path drops the entire lane manifest/receipt instead of recording a bounded failure and retaining remaining evidence. (3) LOW: fixture-signed-copy is inferred by a negative substring rule (src/rs9/package_roles.py:88-93); the real failure path lane-work/quarantine/<name>.rpm (unsigned, construction-verified candidate; src/rs9/hosted_packaging.py:629-638, collected at src/rs9/hosted_pipeline.py:229) is labelled fixture-signed-copy/fixture-test-only. No authority upgrade, but binary/fixture distinguishability is not exact. (4) LOW: validate_product_architectures now checks only binary-package rows (src/rs9/hosted_summary.py:33-35); fixture-signed copies (what client gates install) are no longer checked against the lane's expected system. RPM copies only get header/filename self-consistency and membership in {x86_64,aarch64,noarch}; pacman fixture copies get no type check. (5) LOW: the run-14 manifests fixture is used only for the historical architecture diagnostic and authority rejection; no test replays the exact run-14 custody path set (lane-work/unsigned, unsigned-custody/files, rpm/fedora/43/<arch>/Packages fixture copies, unsigned-source) through producer_role/retain and checks that roles and promotions match the historical promotions. The retain->summarize integration test uses 'unsigned/' without the lane-work prefix and omits the fixture and unsigned-custody copies. (6) NITS: operators/live1/run-hosted15.py is recorded mode 100644 versus 100755 for run-hosted10..14 (works via the documented python3 invocation); the diagnostic message 'TLS verification failed: <token>' is also emitted for handshake/protocol/eof classes (src/rs9/readers.py:671,683; src/rs9/pages_origin.py:281).",
